                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "The last of Grybanser Fox's [b]Zeno[/b] games has battle mechas instead of ships, due to an important new feature: every other stage, they land and the action turns into multidirectional, free-roaming shooting, as you look for objectives to destroy in order to open a passage to the following section of the stage. The firing direction is locked by holding the button, just like they did before twin-stick controls became the norm.\n\nThe scoring mechanics introduced in [b]Jet Buster[/b] have been mostly maintained. Enemies release coins that raise the level of medals, and of course more and better medals collected are worth more points at the end of a stage. The significant difference is that now coins can't be turned into medals, the latter exclusively come out of enemies destroyed with the secondary weapon, which is generally quite weaker than the primary; alternate use of both weapons is necessary.\n\nMost notably, there are no more unlocks or shops, so medals are just for scoring. If you decide to prioritize completion over high score, you may not collect a single medal during a run, in favor of accumulating coins that can be spent as credits to continue. If you play cool and safe over the first two or three levels, you are likely set for further on.\n\nWhile decent, the graphics can't shake a certain amateurish and \"blocky\" feel at times, and they can't hold a candle to [b]Super XYX[/b], which the team co-developed and released just a few weeks earlier. It's not a simple matter of visual style and detail, though, as due to some questionable coloring choices and some backgrounds too busy with distracting details, [i]especially[/i] in Stage 4, the action gets occasionally confusing, and making out platforms from backgrounds in multidirectional stages is often harder than it should be.\n\nEven so, I could appreciate the developers' effort to craft this \"grand finale\" for their series, with a clearly bigger scope and ambition, if not fully realized. Over time, they learned to make their games less daunting to new players, and this is no exception despite the loss of meta-progression elements.\nWhile the story is really thin, involving some friendly higher entity and time travel to resolve the great war against the alien invaders, the ending is notable for being, in my eyes, oddly optimistic yet bleak at the same time.\n\nAfter a productive half decade, the developers have stopped making games, but have kept updating the old ones.",
